#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9514d00d-1628-526b-bfb9-3922bfa89296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122477} {:answer/id #uuid "9830b05e-b1e5-5472-a687-d858d5ea55a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122478} {:answer/id #uuid "acc8b462-ca6a-5540-91ff-bd580a56d550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122479} {:answer/id #uuid "1805d7fe-61c3-57eb-b65a-e65d028ccd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122480} {:answer/id #uuid "448ee7d1-a5b2-54d4-959d-3a593c75ad96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9514d00d-1628-526b-bfb9-3922bfa89296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122477} :answer-field/id #uuid "884d8a23-2904-5338-8504-2d83038763a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5f682f-a0de-52f6-930d-b54c3a22eede" :question/math-academy-id "q-36488" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c59446c-928f-5c4a-a62e-55383c3a3ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122723} {:answer/id #uuid "a21ed5ec-9dcc-5453-8bec-584bcfacb2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122724} {:answer/id #uuid "ad06bd27-d360-57d5-8aac-8cbdd901efc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122725} {:answer/id #uuid "0c597ed8-f192-52ee-bb1b-9d221a5cfeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122726} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf1219e-9c23-569a-9bab-7217d89bfcd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf1219e-9c23-569a-9bab-7217d89bfcd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122727} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0588b70-d4a8-599c-9f59-3d0e36025fe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "894b16d3-3e6a-5bd5-b6ff-86b2b95aa0cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37154" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "796e8f6a-c08b-5e85-b44d-1815f11ece67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384308} {:answer/id #uuid "8192ce68-0b23-5cbe-8693-5c8ee81ff92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384309} {:answer/id #uuid "29046886-e92a-528d-b87d-f656da91227d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384310} {:answer/id #uuid "1c427bec-b70c-5341-87e2-12a7c64dda21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384311} {:answer/id #uuid "b08e7ce6-ab00-5fce-b07a-accab2990b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "796e8f6a-c08b-5e85-b44d-1815f11ece67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384308} :answer-field/id #uuid "4523064d-41ad-564c-aee2-06f74cb69951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fdb130b3-f386-5a0d-a67b-84c93acff686" :question/math-academy-id "q-82614" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c616a8c6-5f50-5deb-ad69-15fcd020c0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384315} {:answer/id #uuid "223b2510-4a91-5872-b337-7b239ede4f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384316} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff572d7-558c-5225-9f64-64e7d1b0971d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384317} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3fd2ca-7531-5b90-bca4-818723535a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384318} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4aeef9-1c15-5088-afdc-995c51ea30c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff572d7-558c-5225-9f64-64e7d1b0971d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384317} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7f04cf-c5a1-5722-bd71-5614006be7b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a7ee4f4-73c6-5824-a327-bfe7ce4e304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82707" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4bd8f5e-192f-5ef0-94df-d81de793e01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384322} {:answer/id #uuid "5835e931-8ee9-5f62-ad12-798f8c51fc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384323} {:answer/id #uuid "58878980-24e0-5107-bcdb-7c32a5a68d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384324} {:answer/id #uuid "1403fe75-03c0-5c2c-9e2b-ad7fdedcce76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384325} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0521bd-6c6b-5506-8c53-5ce32e76aa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0521bd-6c6b-5506-8c53-5ce32e76aa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384326} :answer-field/id #uuid "d311ca08-07f4-5c99-9836-2ef897bc25dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69f19ddc-b771-5d32-afa2-60c3aee0640c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82718" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
